WEBVTT
NOTE Follow-up 3 of 3 — renewal outcome, 2026-11-19 09:30 CET (~3 min)

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:08.000
David: Priya! Your calendar note literally said "smugness call, 15 minutes". I'm assuming good news.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:19.000
Priya: Insufferably smug, as promised. Signed Tuesday. Two-year term, API bundled, net cost up four percent on the old contract, which against the original quote is — I did the math for my own satisfaction — a very good outcome.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:26.000
David: Congratulations, honestly. What tipped it in the end?

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Priya: The KPI, without question. Once the CFO's office made time-to-takedown a standing monthly number, the API stopped being my convenience and became their data lineage question. Procurement doesn't care what Diego suffers, but "our board metric depends on an unsupported spreadsheet process" — that sentence they cared about. I wrote it knowing exactly what it would do, ha.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.000
David: And Diego? Week one, like you predicted?

00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:04.000
Priya: Day two. The dashboard is alive again, Ana's old one, resurrected. He sent the team a screenshot with the caption "join_hell_v7.xlsx has been retired with full honors". The December one-pagers are already scheduled queries. He's now bored, which is its own management problem.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:12.000
David: Ha! Okay — while I have you. When we first talked you said you spend more time explaining the tool's value than the tool spends explaining itself. Still true?

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:31.000
Priya: Less true, but for a funny reason — nothing about the product's reporting changed. What changed is we now own the pipeline, so the explaining got cheaper. The gap I'd still name: everything we built is about our internal story. There's still nothing I'd put in front of a customer-facing team, like — sales keeps asking for a "brand protection health" view per key account, and that's still screenshots and goodwill.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:38.000
David: Who asked for that, specifically? Was that the same VP ecommerce?

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Priya: No, new voice — the key accounts director. She wants to show big retail partners we police their channel. Different audience, different number. I've done two of those decks by hand this quarter. If you ever want that conversation, it's hers, not mine — I can introduce you, she's chronically online, she'll say yes.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:58.000
David: I'd love that intro. And in January when the per-region KPI goes live — can I see how the first month actually goes?

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:08.000
Priya: Yes, but February — January will be on fire, it always is. Book something mid-Feb with me and Diego. If the KPI survives its first board appearance, drinks are on me.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.000
David: Mid-Feb it is. Enjoy the smugness, you earned it.
